    "updated_at": "2026-11-18T21:57:03Z",
    "body": "Reopened. The maintainer still sees the defect in a build that has this work (2026-11-18). His words:\n\n> The issue her is that the trochoidal for a large area keeps it tight within the tool diameter when it should try to sweep as much of the open area as possible.. this is setup as a outsided containment.. and it ggenerates ALOT of uncessary movement\n\nHe sent a screenshot. It is not attached here, because it shows his project. What it shows:\n\n- A 2D Adaptive operation, Tool containment Outside, on a large rectangular stock area round a part with two bosses and a waist.\n- The area is large and open. The Trochoid loops stay about one tool diameter wide.\n- The loops cover the whole stock face in many tight circles. The result is a very large number of moves and many small plunges and retracts.\n\nStatus line of the operation (quoted): `generated 2D Adaptive1 Copy Copy: 6270 moves (12 plunge(s) @ 300, 4 level(s), 2250 arc(s)), 200 warning(s) - adaptive: the HSM plan is refused: the area is a channel 46.7 mm wide and ~316 mm long (the band round a part?) ... a trochoid the area is no single channel (2 loops about its medial line) - Offset rings cut this pocket instead`.\n\nExpected: in a wide open area, each trochoid loop sweeps as much of the free width as the engagement limit permits. It does not stay at one tool diameter. The path has far fewer moves.\n\nNext steps for the worker:\n1. Reproduce with a headless fixture: an Outside containment on a wide stock ring round a part with a waist. Record the move count, the loop width and the engagement.\n2. Find why the loop width stays tight: the width rule, the engagement cap, or the fallback to Offset rings after the HSM plan and the trochoid are refused. Name which one produced the path in the screenshot.\n3. Make the loop width follow the free width, within the engagement and tool-load limits. Keep the no-gouge rules.\n4. Pin the fix with a test that checks the move count and the loop width. The 2D toolpath shape-mask tests apply.\n\nThis is a user-reported defect, so the issue is now P3 and `bug`. The old `P4` label is removed. Related: #220, #5, #47 (trochoid design and open items), #1950 (budget refusal in the 3D Pocket).\n",
    "author_association": "OWNER",
